  "final_text": "I've created the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled and has already run once successfully.\n\n**1. What the data holds**\n\nThe data view has about 14,000 web requests. Each one records the time, client IP, URL, response code, response size in bytes, source and destination country, the user's operating system and browser, referrer and file extension.\n- **Response codes:** only three appear: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n- **Time span:** the data runs from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, so most of it is dated in the future. Older parts of it scroll in as time passes.\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-65c8e895 Web logs overview\"** (id `06390061-3fef-44d3-85e1-0f84d612fb62`)\n\nIt has eight panels and is set to the last 7 days:\n- Four headline numbers: total requests, unique visitors (by client IP), average response size, and the share of requests that failed with a 4xx or 5xx code (8.82% across all the data).\n- Requests over time, stacked by response code.\n- The 10 most-requested URLs and the 10 countries sending the most traffic.\n- Total bytes sent over time.\n\nI ran the error-rate query and a count by response code directly against the data, and both returned the numbers above. I haven't opened the dashboard in a browser, so the other panels' queries haven't been run and I haven't seen how the charts look.\n\n**3. Rule: \"EFF-65c8e895 5xx spike\"** (id `f9ac6423-1ac1-4f93-a920-5aa0ee90b88f`)\n- It checks every minute and fires when more than 10 requests with a response code from 500 to 599 arrive within the last 5 minutes.\n- **One change from the default:** Kibana turns on a setting that ignores requests already counted in the previous check. With a 1-minute check over a 5-minute window, that would stop it from being a true 5-minute count, so I turned it off.\n\n**Decision for you:** the rule has no notification set up, so when it fires it only shows an alert inside Kibana. If you want an email or Slack message, tell me where to send it and I'll add it.",
